wordlist.h - struct definitions and function declarations for word list
tokenizer.c - reads files using open/read and extracts words
tokenizer.h - tokenizer function declarations
traverse.c - recursively traverses directories and collects valid files
traverse.h - traversal function declarations
compare.c - computes JSD and sorts/prints results
compare.h - comparison structures and function declarations
Makefile - builds compare executable
AUTHOR - NetIDs in this file
README - README file

Program design
--------------
Word Frequency Distribution (WFD):
Each file is represented as a linked list of nodes.
Each node contains:
word (string)
count (integer)
next pointer

When a word is read:
search list for the word
increment count if found
otherwise insert new node

After reading the file:
counts are converted into frequencies by dividing by total word count

Tokenization:
Files are read using POSIX system calls:
open(), read(), close()
Files are processed using buffered reads for efficiency.

Words are defined as sequences of:
letters, numbers, and hyphen (-)
All other characters are ignored
Words are separated by whitespace
Words are converted to lowercase for case-insensitivity
No maximum word length is assumed (dynamic allocation used)

Directory traversal:
Uses opendir(), readdir(), closedir()
Recursively explores directories

Rules:
files passed as arguments are always included
only files ending in ".txt" are included from directories
hidden files/directories (starting with '.') are ignored

Jensen-Shannon Distance (JSD):
For each pair of files:
compute frequency distributions F1 and F2
compute mean distribution F(w) = 1/2 (F1(w) + F2(w))
compute KLD for each file
compute JSD = sqrt(1/2 KLD(F1||F) + 1/2 KLD(F2||F))
Missing words are treated as frequency 0
Uses log2() and sqrt() from math library

Analysis phase:
All file pairs are compared (unordered pairs)
For each pair:
compute JSD
compute combined word count
Results stored in an array of structs

Sorted using qsort():
Results are sorted in decreasing order of combined word count so comparisons involving larger files appear first.

Testing
-------
Correctness tests:
Identical files -> JSD = 0
Completely different files -> JSD = 1

Tokenization tests:
Case-insensitivity (Hello vs hello)
Punctuation handling ("can't", "thieves'")
Hyphen handling ("semi-auto")
Numeric tokens ("123", "cs214")
Whitespace edge cases

Edge cases:
Empty files
Very long words (1000+ characters)
Repeated words with different frequencies

File handling:
Single file input (error case)
Nonexistent file (perror handling)
Recursive directory traversal
Suffix filtering (.txt only)
Hidden files/directories ignored
Mixed file and directory inputs

Sorting:
Verified ordering by combined word count

Memory safety:
Tested using AddressSanitizer and UBSan
No memory leaks or undefined behavior detected

Notes
-----
Program prints one comparison per unordered pair

Output format:
JSD file1 file2

Errors are reported using perror() and execution continues

Program exits with error if fewer than two valid files are found
